    "q-162649": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 22",
      "problem": "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $(t+1)^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n2t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
