    "q-14614": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 30",
      "problem": "The equation of a curve is $xy+{x}^{2}=1.$ The normal line at the point $(1,\\,0)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(1,\\,0).$   Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2x & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(2x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,0)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2x+y}{x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+0}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(1,\\,0)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
