    "q-95813": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 42",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼B(200,0.4),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X>90).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9452\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =80 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.4) \\\\  & =120 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(200)(0.4)=80\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(200)(0.4)(1-0.4)=48.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(80,48).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>90).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>90)=P(X\\ge 91)≈P(Y>90.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>90.5) & =P(Z>\\frac{90.5-80}{\\sqrt{48}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.52) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.52) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.52) \\\\  & =1-0.9357 \\\\  & =0.0643\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>90)≈0.064.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0648.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
