    "q-44824": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 46",
      "problem": "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\n- $5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,…$\n- ${a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=18,\\,n\\ge 1$\n- $f(n+1)=2f(n)+3,\\,f(1)=1,\\,n\\ge 1$",
      "worked_solution": "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{10}{5} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)}=\\frac{15}{10} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is a geometric sequence. The recursive rule ${a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n}$ tells us that each term is $6$ times the previous term, so the common ratio is $r=6.$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =1 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\cdot 1+3=5 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\cdot 5+3=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{5}{1} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)}=\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is a geometric sequence.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
