    "q-13589": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 54",
      "problem": "If $f(x)=-2x+1$ and $g(x)=-3x-7,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(-2x+1)-(-3x-7) \\\\  & =(-2x-(-3x))+(1-(-7)) \\\\  & =x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
